Option 1: Notification Trigger + Semantic View Pull
In this approach, when loan data is ready in the HLADS Curated Zone, HLADS would publish a lightweight "trigger" event to the Kafka topic �. This message would only serve as a notification (e.g., "loan data received for this seller") and would not contain the actual loan details �.
The Process: The downstream LPO application would consume this trigger, recognize the event, and then actively reach back into HLADS through a "semantic view" to pull the 200+ data elements into its own database �.
The Drawback: This requires a two-step "status and pull" process, increasing the complexity of the interaction �.
Option 2: Full Data Payload (Pub/Sub Model)
In this approach, the actual loan data itself is embedded directly within the Kafka event message �.
The Process: When data reaches the Curated Zone, HLADS generates an event that includes all available data fields (which could range from 80 to 200+ fields depending on the seller) formatted as a MISMO JSON payload �. LPO simply subscribes to the topic and ingests the data as it arrives without needing to query HLADS �.
The Advantages: The primary speaker strongly advocated for this approach because it is a single-step process (one notification that gets the exact data needed) �. Furthermore, Kafka's in-motion encryption ensures that transmitting this sensitive data within the payload is secure �.
The Rationale for the Action Item
A critical reason for favoring the Full Payload approach is bidirectional symmetry. If the team uses a MISMO JSON payload to send data downstream, they can use the exact same JSON format to sync data back upstream �. If a user updates 10 fields in the downstream application, the system can simply send a JSON payload back to HLADS with those 10 fields populated and the rest left blank �.
Ashish took the action item to review this proposed Pub/Sub architecture with decision-makers "Push" and "Sai" to ensure they fully support this two-way traffic model �. The team set a deadline to finalize this decision by their next Thursday meeting �. The urgency is because if leadership chooses Option 1 (the semantic view pull), the team does not need to spend time designing a MISMO JSON format for the Kafka payload; but if they choose Option 2, designing that JSON format is the immediate next step �.

